    historyContent: {
      origins: "The Tarock family emerged in Northern Italy during the Early Renaissance (first evidenced 1440). From the mid-18th century in the German-speaking world and Habsburg monarchy, reforms added bidding (from L'Hombre and Quadrille), the 54-card pack, talon exchange, and the bonus when the lowest trump (Trump I) wins the last trick. Königrufen first appears in 1827 as a four-player variant of Tapp Tarock in an appendix to the Tarok Tappen rulebook; the first book devoted solely to Königrufen was published in Vienna in 1840. The game boomed between 1880 and 1920 and gained more contracts throughout the 20th century. It is played across Austria and South Tyrol and by 2015 had become the favourite card game of Austrians.",
      originCountries: [
        'Austria',
      ],
      timeline: [
        '1827: First written description of Königrufen (Tarok Tappen appendix).',
        '1840: First book devoted to Königrufen published in Vienna.',
        '1880–1920: Boom period; four-bid structure and growth of regional play.',
        '1990s: Regular tournament series begin.',
        '2008: Annual Austrian Final (Österreich-Finale) established.',
        '2024: UNESCO Intangible Cultural Heritage status.',
      ],
      evolution: 'Evolved from Tapp Tarock with partner-calling from Quadrille; 20th-century addition of negative contracts (e.g. Piccolo) and bird bonuses; today 13 contracts and 8 bonuses in Austrian Final rules.',
      cultural: 'National card game of Austria; stronghold in Upper Austria; viewed in Austrian law as a game of skill; annual championship in Casino Linz.',
    },
